{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli after his death in 1152.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was born around 1140. His father Raymond II was a cousin of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem and a prominent military leader in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond III's early life is not well documented but it is known that he succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at a relatively young age.\n\nRaymond III played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 1160s. In 1164 he was captured by the forces of Nur ad-Din Zengi during the Battle of Harim. He was released in 1170 after a ransom was paid. After his release Raymond III became a key advisor to King Amalric I of Jerusalem.\n\n*The Regency of Raymond III*\nIn 1174 King Amalric I died and was succeeded by his son Baldwin IV. However Baldwin IV was a minor and Raymond III was appointed as regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond III's regency lasted for several years during which time he played a crucial role in the defense of the kingdom against the forces of Saladin.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin*\nIn 1187 Raymond III played a key role in the Battle of Hattin. The battle was a decisive victory for Saladin and resulted in the capture of King Guy of Lusignan and many other prominent nobles. Raymond III escaped from the battle and fled to Tripoli where he died later that year.\n\nRaymond III was succeeded as Count of Tripoli by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. He was remembered as a skilled military leader and a key figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the 12th century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.005094549153000116}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token":